---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureCompressToSize from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfSecureCompressToSize.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-pdf-to-200kb">
  <PdfSecureCompressToSize client:load targetBytes={204800} />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Who asks for 200 KB</h2>
      <p>
        After 100 KB, 200 KB is the most common hard limit on document uploads. You see it on
        recruitment portals that take a CV and certificates, exam boards that collect mark sheets,
        insurance claim forms, and the "supporting documents" step of many public-sector
        applications. Many Indian competitive-exam systems publish limits between 50 KB and 200 KB per
        attachment.
      </p>
      <p>
        200 KB gives about one full quality step more than 100 KB, which is enough for a two- or
        three-page document to stop looking compressed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What doubling the budget actually changes</h2>
      <p>
        JPEG size does not scale linearly with quality, so twice the bytes is not twice the detail. On
        the same document, 200 KB instead of 100 KB gives one of these:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>about 40 more DPI at the same JPEG quality: 150 DPI instead of 110;</li>
        <li>
          or about 15–20 points of JPEG quality at the same resolution, which removes visible blocking;
        </li>
        <li>or twice as many pages with the same appearance.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        By default the tool chooses: resolution first, so text stays sharp, then quality. If you want
        a softer page at a higher resolution, change it in the resolution options.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>A checklist for application uploads</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Read the specification again. Portals often set a separate, smaller limit for the photograph
          and signature. A 200 KB document limit does not mean the photo can be 200 KB.
        </li>
        <li>
          Upload the number of pages they asked for. A ten-page scan when two were requested wastes
          four-fifths of the budget and is often rejected.
        </li>
        <li>
          Keep the file name plain. Some old upload handlers fail on spaces, brackets or non-ASCII
          characters. <code>surname-marksheet.pdf</code> is a safe form.
        </li>
        <li>
          Open the result before you submit. The per-page table gives the numbers; your eyes tell you
          if the registration number is still readable.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>If your source is a photograph rather than a scan</h2>
      <p>
        Phone photos of documents compress badly. They contain uneven lighting, a gradient across the
        page, camera noise in the shadows and a background. JPEG spends bytes on all this
        high-frequency detail, which you do not want.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Use the document scanner built into your phone, not the camera app. It corrects the
          perspective and removes the background.
        </li>
        <li>
          Photograph the page on a plain, evenly lit surface with no shadow across it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Turn on grayscale here if the ink is black. Colour noise on a white page wastes budget.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>No queue, no daily limit, no account</h2>
      <p>
        In application season, you often compress the same documents several times as you find the
        rules of each portal. Upload-based compressors often allow two or three files a day on the free
        tier, and they count each retry. Here the encoder runs in your browser, so the twentieth attempt
        costs the same as the first.
      </p>
      <p>
        If 200 KB is the wrong target, the same engine is available at
        <a href="/compress-pdf-to-100kb">100 KB</a>, <a href="/compress-pdf-to-300kb">300 KB</a>,
        <a href="/compress-pdf-to-500kb">500 KB</a> or
        <a href="/compress-pdf-to-size">any size you type in</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
